#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222370} :activity/id #uuid "1dec0b3b-2ae8-5b26-a304-6a22eea83fad" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049492 :topic/id #uuid "f922a910-dc10-5692-b2c2-fa166217d40c" :topic/math-academy-id 6370} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222526 :tutorial/content "The **unit step function** $u(t-a)$ acts like a switch:\n\n- If $t < a$, then $u(t-a)=0$.\n\n- If $t \\ge a$, then $u(t-a)=1$.\n\nSo, when we multiply a function $g(t)$ by $u(t-a)$, the function becomes\n![](images/99/99f4e68acca148ef5e6c69f20bba890868d3dbe8e75f0616e77ba5fc1bb27d87.svg)\n\nThis operation \"switches on\" the function $g(t)$ at time $t=a$.\n\nFor example, consider the function $y(t)=u(t-2) \\cdot \\dfrac14 t^2.$ Here, $a=2$ and $g(t) = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2,$ so\n![](images/b4/b4f287198bb074d3a90853f3418e5f606d7ec022770eef76ebbd5b54aeb60aec.svg)\nThe plot of this function is shown below. Note that the curve follows the parabola for $t \\ge 2$ but is zero for $t < 2$.\n\n![](images/04/04398ba96ee11b0f99405b6890ec2c16070460e45bbe7aec1630ec27698d0b93.png)\n\nSuppose we know the Laplace transform of $g(t).$ We'll now learn how to find the Laplace transform of $u(t-a)\\,g(t).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4a22f9b9-23e6-503b-9c4d-1b5a100a6aa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14741 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "76a7d3b3-6b52-5a8e-bdef-581b35cb59f7" :step/math-academy-id 600720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222477 :tutorial/content "From the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}\n=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}u(t-a)g(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nBecause $u(t-a)=0$ for $t < a$ and $u(t-a)=1$ for $t\\ge a$, the integrand is zero until $t=a$.\nSo the integral reduces to\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}\n=\\int_a^\\infty e^{-st}g(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo compare this with a standard Laplace transform, we shift the variable of integration so that the lower limit is $0$.\n\nLet $\\tau=t-a$. Then $t=\\tau+a$ and $\\textrm{d}t=\\textrm{d}\\tau$. Also, when $t=a$, we have $\\tau=0$.\n\nSubstituting gives\n\n$$\n\\int_a^\\infty e^{-st}g(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s(\\tau+a)}g(\\tau+a)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau.\n$$\n\nNow separate the exponential term:\n\n$$\ne^{-s(\\tau+a)}=e^{-as}e^{-s\\tau}.\n$$\n\nThis allows us to factor out the constant $e^{-as}$:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s(\\tau+a)}g(\\tau+a)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau\n=e^{-as}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s\\tau}g(\\tau+a)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau.\n$$\n\nThe remaining integral has exactly the form of a Laplace transform, so recognize\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-s\\tau}g(\\tau+a)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau\n=\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nPutting everything together, we obtain the **second shifting theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}\n=e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t+a)\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for } s>s_0\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway is that the unit step shifts the *start time* of the function to $t=a$, and changing variables shifts it back to $0$. The exponential factor $e^{-as}$ records this delay in the $s$-domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "78179940-a5e4-5e62-a269-1f11e9e978bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14334 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "58c845a7-9932-5d1d-a0fc-9aba84e9545e" :step/math-academy-id 600721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221156 :question/id #uuid "41b1a655-6092-57b6-8f95-396c566e7a83" :question/math-academy-id "e-21445"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050458}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552482}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "f08f8a01-3d83-5e75-825e-84b7186234ec" :step/math-academy-id 600722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222373}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221166 :question/id #uuid "88ee94b3-1f6e-587a-a63f-736f9df7186e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21582"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050986}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552515}] :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "042651a6-d9a0-5ceb-abae-96f44f3f75db" :step/math-academy-id 600725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222374}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222374 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222524 :tutorial/content "We begin with the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a) g(t)\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nIn many applications, the function multiplied by the unit step already appears in the shifted form\n\n$$\nu(t-a)\\,f(t-a).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to rewrite the theorem so that this structure can be handled directly.\n\nSuppose $f(t)$ is a function with Laplace transform\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).\n$$\n\nTo match the form $u(t-a)f(t-a)$, define\n\n$$\ng(t)=f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThen the left-hand side becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a) g(t)\\}\n=\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, determine what happens to the right-hand side.\nIf $g(t)=f(t-a)$, then\n\n$$\ng(t+a)=f\\big((t+a)-a\\big)=f(t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t+a)\\}=\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).\n$$\n\nSubstituting these results into the theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\}\n= e^{-as}F(s).\n$$\n\nThis is the **alternative form of the second shifting theorem**.\n\nNext, we will apply this formula to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4b75df16-a3fb-58a6-80a7-1bd34712e16e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14716 :tutorial/title "Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "39287031-f3bc-53dd-a472-3a996c44f0d7" :step/math-academy-id 600728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221442 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221205 :question/id #uuid "1b27851d-3630-506e-972c-b6cef8dbeae3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21864"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552526}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "1f6fcaea-7f50-52de-86e2-2be948ab6086" :step/math-academy-id 600729}] :activity/title "The Second Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221463}]}
